what do stoics do when others behave  badly this is a great question to ask  and it does need an answer it comes up a  lot in stoic forums and groups in  workshops in random questions that get  posted on videos and it sometimes comes  in a very generic formulation like the  one that I just gave you and sometimes  it's with a great amount of precision  and specificity as the person gives  details about the bad or wrong actions  of other people that either they are  observing or they're themselves being  affected by and it's a great question in  part because what it reveals is a  mistaken understanding that sometimes  arises about stoicism  a an understanding that isn't completely  wrong but which takes a stoic idea and  then blows it up into all of stoicism  and that is of the sort of interior  withdrawal going into yourself uh  against a hostile world of both events  and other people that you feel could be  detrimental to you and it's aided by you  know little quips from you know whether  Marcus Aurelius or Seneca or Epictetus  you know for example saying you're not  being insulted unless you choose to view  yourself as being insulted nobody can  really harm you the whole notion of the  Interior Citadel and while there's some  truth to that that's not the totality of  stoicism what is the genuine stoic  approach to seeing bad or wrong or  irrational actions being done by other  people there's really three main parts  to it you can could say two or three  depending on how things are being  directed because it could be directed at  um you know the environment or animals  people you don't know the world in  general the bad actions it could be uh  directed towards beings that you feel  some sort of identification or concern  or care for remember that stoicism holds  that we are social communal animals and  that's part of our rationality and that  uh it is quite natural that we take care  of others who are not ourselves or it  could be directed at us and so in the  case of the second and third we would  have three parts to this so what are the  the general parts of a stoic approach to  seeing uh bad behavior on the part of  other people one part is classifying the  action as a bad button that takes some  doing  we also would say that you know we could  you can there's a lot of ways to  formulate this Epictetus says you can  tell yourself the action seems right or  good to that person who's doing the  wrong thing and we could say good right  or reasonable these are all closely  connected and then we can also say that  the third part is realizing that it  doesn't have to affect you negatively  unless you choose to allow it to do so  and that has to do with your own  responses and mindset so let's look at  the first one first right being stoic  does not mean avoiding judgments or not  being even judgmental it means making  judgments rightly about moral matters  which extend not just to good or bad  right or wrong but also two reasonable  and unreasonable prudence is very  important so I was saying to somebody  man that's that's really stupid what  you're doing that's a judgment and it  could in fact be  a proper judgment right if the action is  indeed a stupidly set up one or stupidly  motivated one  um stoicism does not mean saying that  nothing outside of the core of yourself  has value and you can think about the  different criteria that stoics typically  appeal to the cardinal virtues of wisdom  Justice courage and Temperance and the  corresponding vices provides moral  criteria to say that something is good  or bad right and a person has virtues or  vices or behaves virtuously or viciously  whether  um their actions whether their mindset  is in accordance with nature understood  in its full sense to include a properly  developed human nature or whether  they're out of a chord or against nature  that's another main criteria another  absolutely Central criteria the notion  of it can be trained translated Duty or  appropriate actions  this is really Central to stoicism as  well and to be a stoic doesn't mean to  stop using those criteria or to only  apply them to yourself and you know the  stoics will say that being bad or doing  bad actions Behaving Badly is bad for  the person who does them but that  doesn't mean that it's not bad for other  people if you're bullying somebody  because you are insecure and you need a  Target that person that you're bullying  is being negatively affected by it even  if it's just in terms of you know losing  money or social status they're probably  not a stoic and you're probably harming  them psychologically right and perhaps  creating More Bullies as a result and a  stoic should have no problem actually  saying that that's the case in the  second part  saying that you know the person who's  Behaving Badly is doing so because that  course of action appears to be right or  reasonable or good to them you know one  of the things that we want to think  about our our emotional reactions and  the thoughts that they stem from so  Epictetus is a great example in this as  is Seneca who's got an entire work on  this emotion anger is a very common  response to seeing people behaving badly  and it's not a good thing from a stoic  perspective it stems from our own  insecurity our own mistaken conceptions  of things and so you know we want to  avoid anger and other excessive emotions  Epictetus actually does talk about uh an  emotion that in Greek is a Laos which is  often translated as pity we could also  translate it with a word that we like  more these days because pity has a  pejorative connotation compassion right  it's you can feel compassion towards  this poor a bastard who is so screwed up  in their you know thoughts and emotional  structures and habits and assumptions  that they get basic things wrong in this  fear of morality and action and think  that they're doing the right thing when  they're doing the wrong thing right and  sometimes these Rights and Wrongs can  have several different levels and  there's nothing that says as a stoic you  can't try to persuade somebody that  their course of action is wrong you can  even try to compel them to do the right  thing that's probably going to be  unsuccessful you can inform them you can  exhort them you can do all sorts of  things and you can also Shield people  who are going to be negatively affected  there's nothing in stoicism that says  that you shouldn't do that and actually  there's good reason to think that you  ought to do those sorts of things when  you're seeing somebody on the loose with  a whole bunch of wrong-headed ideas in  their head leading to bad behavior  coming to the last thing so when it's  affecting you or somebody that you're  close to that you identify with that you  care about you have a choice about how  you allow these things to affect you and  whether you do allow them to affect you  in the core of who you are some of the  stoics call this the ruling part the  hegemony cone in Greek some like  Epictetus will talk in terms of Prior  racist The Faculty of choice also  translated in a variety of other ways  like choice or moral character various  other things but the the idea is that  you use that part of yourself to decide  how that part of yourself is going to be  affected so if you're going to let  yourself get bent out of shape as Seneca  points out every time that somebody does  something bad boy are you gonna be an  upset person right  um and it doesn't mean by the way that  people can't affect us in all sorts of  externals from our bodies to our social  status to our money and income and  wealth  um our relationships somebody can spread  gossip about us and you know a  friendship winds up being destroyed in  the process all of that is true we are  we are vulnerable to those sorts of  things it's just that those from the  stoic perspective don't have the kind of  value that being a virtuous or vicious  person does  um we're not impervious and we s